<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takua Pa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takua Pa is a town and district in Phang Nga Province on the Andaman Sea coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. In the first millennium CE the area, especially the island of Ko Kho Khao, was a port on the route across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. A ninth-century Tamil inscription found there names a merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> and a tank named after a title of the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-iii">Nandivarman III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takua-pa.jpg" alt="Takua Pa" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hdamm based on Amphoe Phang Nga.svg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphoe_8205.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The district lies on the Andaman Sea coast, with Si Phang Nga National Park to the north and Khao Lak-Lam Ru National Park, which includes the beach resorts of Khao Lak, in the south. Its neighbours are Khura Buri to the north, Phanom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat-thani">Surat Thani</a> province and Kapong to the east, and Thai Mueang to the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was first named Takola and was also known by the Tamil name Takkolam, a word for piper cubeba; it may have abounded in such <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, been settled from the place of the same name in the Tamil country, or both. It is said to have been the finest harbour on the west coast of the peninsula for trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and the Tamil kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and Pallavas.</p><p>Finds at Ban Thung Tuek resemble the Tang ceramics, pottery from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and glassware from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> found at Laem Pho across the isthmus, suggesting an overland route by which traders could avoid piracy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> temple stood there, for which the merchant body Kodumbalur Manigramam built a tank, recording the work in Tamil.</p><p>The later name Takua Pa comes from takua, the Thai word for lead, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> was the most important ore found nearby. Long administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a>, the town became a province in 1892, part of Monthon Phuket. The province was merged into Phang Nga Province on 1 April 1932, and the district of Talat Yai was renamed Takua Pa. In the first half of the 20th century it was an important tin-dredging area, worked by the English Siamese Tin Syndicate and the Australian Satupulo No Liability Co. with narrow-gauge tramways along the rivers; Thai firms had taken over by the early 1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>By 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> plantations covered the former dredged land, and the public library in Takua Pa town displays photographs of the dredging of the 1920s and 1930s. The centre for identifying the bodies found after the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> tsunami, which devastated Khao Lak, is in the district, with a cemetery for unidentified foreign victims. The district is divided into eight sub-districts and 51 villages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Takua Pa district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…such as Mamallapuram to Sri Lanka, Southeast Asia and China, carried by merchant guilds; a Tamil inscription at <b>Takua Pa</b> in southern Thailand, which mentions a tank named after a title of Nandivarman III and a merchant guild, the Manigramam, attests to these links across the Bay of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
